body:
## Summary

Create one Usage Explorer that lets a user move from a scoped period summary to the models, projects, days, and sessions that produced it.

The roadmap coordinates filter semantics, explicit date ranges, project/workspace identity, shareable URL state, paginated history, and privacy-safe session detail. It does not change token definitions, source provenance, pricing authority, or unavailable-versus-zero behavior.

## Problem

The dashboard currently exposes useful overview, chart, model, and history surfaces, but they do not yet behave as one exploration flow:

- tool, model, and chart-series interactions use different meanings;
- active scope is not consistently visible across every section;
- explicit start/end dates are unavailable;
- project/workspace identity is not normalized across sources;
- copied URLs do not recreate the selected view;
- history is a fixed recent slice rather than a paginated result set;
- aggregate changes cannot be followed into metadata-first session detail.

This makes it harder to answer: “What caused this usage change?”

## Desired outcome

A single canonical scope applies to Overview, Daily Tokens, Model Breakdown, and Usage History:

- date or named range;
- tool/source;
- model;
- project/workspace when supported;
- privacy-safe session search.

Users can move through:

`period summary → chart segment → filtered rows → session detail`

The active scope remains visible and restorable throughout the flow.

## Visual prototype

> [!NOTE]
> The prototype uses deterministic sample data. It is design guidance, not production behavior or a final API contract.

- **Interactive HTML:** [Usage Explorer prototype](https://github.com/raychrisgdp/coding-agent-usage-dashboard/blob/edd02b199a9b1c7c2b64e7233bb52b6b872af64c/docs/prototypes/usage-explorer.html)
- **Desktop capture:** [1440px prototype](https://github.com/raychrisgdp/coding-agent-usage-dashboard/blob/edd02b199a9b1c7c2b64e7233bb52b6b872af64c/docs/prototypes/usage-explorer-desktop.png)
- **Mobile capture:** [390px prototype](https://github.com/raychrisgdp/coding-agent-usage-dashboard/blob/edd02b199a9b1c7c2b64e7233bb52b6b872af64c/docs/prototypes/usage-explorer-mobile.png)
- **Artifact PR:** #53

![Usage Explorer desktop prototype](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raychrisgdp/coding-agent-usage-dashboard/edd02b199a9b1c7c2b64e7233bb52b6b872af64c/docs/prototypes/usage-explorer-desktop.png)

The prototype demonstrates:

- one canonical filter bar;
- visible removable filter state;
- equal-period comparison and top-driver summary;
- pricing-coverage warning kept separate from subscription spend;
- chart-to-filter drilldown;
- matching Model Breakdown and History scope;
- paginated session results;
- metadata-first session detail with unavailable fields preserved;
- desktop and mobile responsive treatment.

Conceptual details:

- exact visual spacing and sample values;
- final endpoint and query contract;
- production chart library and rendering;
- final project normalization rules.

Expected behavior:

- filter semantics are shared across sections;
- filters can coexist as intersections;
- chart legend controls remain visibility-only;
- copied URLs recreate the selected view;
- chart, model, and history totals reconcile under the same scope;
- drilldown does not expose prompts or message content by default.

## Priority and work lanes

| Priority | Lane | Status | Why now | Hard dependencies |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| P0 | #9 Filter semantics and Model Breakdown scope | Open | Defines the canonical tool/model interaction contract | None; can design against fixtures |
| P0 | #41 Explicit start/end date ranges | Open | Defines one date-window contract across APIs and UI | None; can design against fixtures |
| P1 | Project/workspace identity | Candidate leaf | Adds the missing drilldown dimension without leaking paths | Source capability inventory |
| P1 | Shareable URL and saved view state | Candidate leaf | Makes filtered views reproducible | Frozen canonical filter schema before merge |
| P1 | Paginated history and session detail | Candidate leaf | Completes aggregate-to-evidence exploration | Filter contract and merged pagination contract before merge |
| P2 | Explorer convergence and browser proof | Candidate leaf | Reconciles every surface against one scope | Completion of implementation lanes |

Candidate leaves will be authored separately after their contracts are reviewed. Sharing files or requiring rebases is not, by itself, a native blocker.

## Scope boundaries

In scope:

- one shared scope model across current dashboard sections;
- explicit range controls and validation;
- supported project/workspace metadata;
- URL-persisted state;
- paginated merged history;
- metadata-first session detail;
- scoped export entry point, coordinated with the future export roadmap;
- empty, partial-capability, stale, and error states for Explorer surfaces.

Out of scope:

- prompt or message-content inspection by default;
- live agent control, Kanban, or workflow execution;
- hosted tracing/evaluation infrastructure;
- changing token formulas or pricing authority;
- inferring unavailable project, cost, cache, model, or duration fields;
- treating legend show/hide as a semantic data filter.

## Acceptance criteria

- A copied URL recreates date, tool, model, project, search, and sort state.
- Every aggregate visibly names or inherits the same active scope.
- Tool and model filters can coexist without silently clearing each other.
- Legend controls are visibly separate and affect chart visibility only.
- Selecting a chart day or series opens or applies the corresponding Explorer scope.
- Model Breakdown and History reconcile with the filtered chart totals under documented token semantics.
- Unsupported dimensions are shown as unavailable rather than zero.
- History uses server-side pagination after source merge/sort.
- Session detail is metadata-first and excludes prompt/message content by default.
- Invalid date ranges fail before fetch with an accessible inline message.
- Loading, empty, stale-data, and failure states cross-link to #52 rather than duplicating that roadmap.
- Keyboard, screen-reader, mobile, and reduced-motion constraints cross-link to #17 and #18.
- Browser coverage exercises filter intersections, URL restoration, chart drilldown, pagination, session detail, empty results, and no console errors.

## Documentation and visual alignment

Each implementation leaf owns updates to:

- its public API/filter contract documentation;
- README behavior affected by the leaf;
- the HTML prototype when the approved interaction changes;
- desktop and mobile rendered captures when the prototype changes;
- link checks and visual inspection for any updated artifact.

If a leaf does not affect the prototype, its PR should record that no visual artifact change was required.

## Visual and UX references

- [Claude Usage dashboard screenshot](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/phuryn/claude-usage/3eea154474e93761f774ed38beeaf45baf838a45/docs/screenshot.png): global model/range controls and Overview/Graphs/Tables separation.
- [Lumo overview screenshot](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zhnd/lumo/a51dccd165611bb44f25625df14f1d1968ad2274/docs/images/overview.jpg): compact comparison copy and overview-to-activity hierarchy.
- [CodexScope dashboard screenshot](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/JUk1-GH/CodexScope/21fcc718de232cca7f3453f9156bf8fec1e2aae0/assets/codexscope-dashboard-24h.png): ranked sessions/models and visible range controls.
- [Langfuse repository](https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse): broader aggregate-to-filtered-detail pattern donor; not an architecture template for this local dashboard.

## Related roadmaps and issues

- #52 owns loading and refresh behavior.
- #17 owns chart/input/focus accessibility.
- #18 owns mobile and reduced-motion behavior.
- #43 owns application modularity and code ownership.
- Future Export and Reproducibility work should consume the Explorer filter contract rather than define another scope model.
